  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis plan extends the provided simulated annealing (SA) approach with a multi-strategy optimization that includes:\n- Heavy-tailed mutation distribution (\u00e0 la Powell method)\n- Random crossover (standard arithmetic crossover)\n- Multiple competing fitness metrics\n\nThe main issues with the original SA approach are:\n1. Slow convergence near optimal solution\n2. Inconsistent repair mechanism\n3. Doesn't exploit symmetries in the search space\n\nThe new approach will use:\n1. Evolutionary strategy with heavy-tailed mutations (efficient for continuous optimization)\n2. Repair mechanisms applied only to the first half of h (main function)\n3. Dimensionality reduction through midpoint constraint enforcement\n4. Overlap computation as function of maximum shift\n\nKey innovations:\n- Self-adapting repair scaling factor\n- Progressive cooling schedule\n- Early termination rule\n- Dimensionality reduction by projecting to function space\n- Bayesian-inspired exploration/exploitation with information sharing between peaks\n\nThe rationale is to combine the global exploration power of evolutionary strategies with the constraint handling from Bayesian optimization. The approach directly targets the provided initial solution which already shows promise (0.381277 to target 0.38085).\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: h defined on [0,2] with n_points samples.\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u222bh dx = 1\n- Symmetry h(x) = h(2-x) (imposed by mirroring)\nObjective: \n    C_5 = maxx,k \u222b h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\nBut more precisely, using numpy.correlate with mode='full':\n\n    Let h_full = concatenate([h_prev_half, h_prev_half[::-1]])\n    corr = np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full')\n    C_5 = (np.max(corr) * dx)   [where dx=2.0/n_points]\n\nThis matches the evaluator from the main program.\n\nDimensionality reduction:\n  h_full is determined by first half: h_prev_half\n  We maintain candidate vector of length len(h_prev_half)\n\nConstraints operation:\n  Repair function:\n    current_h = candidate_vector\n    current_sum = sum(current_h)\n    target_sum = 1/(dx * 2) * (2 * len(current_h)) = n_points/4  [from: integral_full = 2 * (sum(current_h)/2 * dx) * dx_scale?] \n    Actually: \n        dx = 2.0/n_points\n        integral_full = 2 * (integral_0_to_1) = 2 * (sum(current_h) * dx / 2) -> This doesn't match the original repair.\n\nDetailed derivation of constraint restoration:\n  Let g = candidate_vector (first half)\n  Then full function h = np.concatenate([g, g[::-1]])\n  Total integral = sum(h) * dx = (2 * sum(g)) * dx = 1\n  => sum(g) = 1/(2*dx) = n_points/4\n\n  So if candidate sum is not n_points/4, we simply scale it:\n    if sum(g) > 0:\n        g = g * ( (n_points/4) / sum(g) )\n    else:\n        g = np.clip(g, 0.001, 0.999)  # ensure not zero\n        g = g * ( (n_points/4) / sum(g) )\n\nThis repair preserves the first half and ensures no division by zero.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1. Initialization:\n  - If 'initial_h_values' is provided, use as starting point\n  - Prep candidate_vector = initial_h_values[:n_points//2]\n  - Repair candidate to meet sum constraint\n  - Set temperature T = 0.6 (initial)\n  - Set cooling rate = 0.995\n\n3.2. Objective evaluation:\n  - Input: candidate_vector (numpy array, first half)\n  - Scale candidate if integral not 1: candidate = repair_vector(candidate)\n  - Build full function: full = np.concatenate([candidate, candidate[::-1]])\n  - Compute correlations: \n        h1 = full.astype(float)\n        h2 = 1 - full.astype(float)\n        corr = np.correlate(h1, h2, mode='full')\n        max_corr = np.max(corr)\n        overlap = max_corr * dx  [dx=2/n_points]\n  - Return {overlap, min, max_vals}   [3 metrics]\n\n3.3. Heavy-tailed mutation operator:\n  For each candidate component:\n    tau = max(0.1, cooling_rate/(1-cooling_rate))\n    eta = 1.5  [mutation noise standard deviation]\n    u = np.random.normal(0, eta)\n    new_component = candidate[i] + u * (max_value - min_value) * tau\n\nBut note: we should also enforce box and integral after mutation.\n\n3.4. Crossover (optional):\n  If rand() < crossover_prob:\n    parent = another_candidate\n    new_vector = np.minimum.outer([rand() < crossover_prob], candidate) * candidate + np.minimum.outer([rand() < crossover_prob], 1-parent) * parent\n\nHowever, given time constraints, we'll implement only mutation.\n\n3.5. Acceptance rule:\n  We'll break the dimension into blocks and mutate independently.\n\nFull algorithm:\n\n  Constants:\n    T0 = 0.6   (initial temperature)\n    alpha = 0.995 (cooling rate)\n    n_steps = 100000 (maximum iterations)\n    dt_budget = 100  (seconds)\n    seed = 42\n\n  Initialize:\n    t0 = time.time()\n    current = candidate_vector  (initial)\n    current_fit = fitness(current)  [overlap, other metrics]\n    best_val = current_fit['overlap']\n    best_vector = current\n\n  For i in range(n_steps):\n    If timing exceeded: break\n\n    # P mutation chance per component (different strategies)\n    p_m = 0.2 (default) + 0.05 * (1 - i/n_steps)  [progressive to exploration]\n\n    new = current.copy()\n    For each position j (out of first_half_length):\n        If random < p_m, mutate it with heavy tail.\n\n        new[j] = current[j] * (1 - 0.3*T) + 0.3*T * (a + b*r)  [this resembles heavy tail]\n\n    repaired_new = repair_vector(new)\n\n    try:\n        f_new = fitness(repaired_new)\n    except:\n        continue\n\n    delta = f_new['overlap'] - current_fit['overlap']\n    if delta <= 0 or exp(-delta/T) > random():\n        current = repaired_new\n        current_fit = f_new\n\n        if f_new['overlap'] < best_val:\n            best_val = f_new['overlap']\n            best_vector = repaired_new\n\n    T = initial_T * alpha^i\n\n  End\n\nTermination checks:\n  - Time budget (100 seconds)\n  - No improvement in 1000 iterations\n  - Random walk adjustment\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n4.1. Initial variant:\n  - Use single heavy-tailed mutation with progressive explore-exploit\n  - Repair by simple scaling\n  - Moderate temperature decay\n\n4.2. Variations:\n  - Tune initial temperature and alpha (cooling rate) from [0.5,0.99] intervals\n  - Add gradient-based stepping occasionally to escape local minima\n  - Change mutation type (Cauchy vs Laplace) for more exploration\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n  - Main time sink: correlation calculation (O(n^2) for small n)\n  - Use early termination if overlap < 0.38 in several checks\n  - Track best overlap every 100 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n  - Converts first_half to full twice during conversion\n  - Re-evaluates final candidate separately\n  - Enforces sum constraint by repair\n  - Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where h_values = np.concatenate([best_vector, best_vector[::-1]])",
